            <ArticleTitle>Headache caused by a sphenoid mucocele but presenting as an ergotamine-induced headache.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>460-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In a 65-year-old woman, symptomatic headache caused by a mucocele of the sphenoid sinus led to ergotamine abuse and subsequent ergotamine-induced headache. Since there were no neurological symptoms initially and the patient previously suffered from migraine, the mucocele was not recognized. Only after unsuccessful drug withdrawal therapy and an MRI, was the correct diagnosis made. Surgical removal of the mucocele led to complete relief of headache within 3 weeks. We conclude that ergotamine-induced headache can develop on the basis of symptomatic headache. In spite of the effectiveness of ergotamine tartrate, an MRI should be performed if focal neurological symptoms occur.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
